        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"direct\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"Section 5.5 'Importance of the Antibiotic Treatment Modality: Molecule and Spectrum' reports on studies comparing antibiotic classes: Ahmed et al. [78] found narrow Gram-positive spectrum antibiotics (vancomycin, daptomycin, linezolid) had no impact on response rate, whereas broad-spectrum antibiotics (β–lactams, quinolones, cyclines) affected response rate and PFS; Chalabi et al. [79] examined several antibiotic classes but found no significantly impairing class for ICI survival; Gaucher et al. [6] found no OS difference between amoxicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanic acid, and other antibiotics. Section 5.1 notes clindamycin (broad anaerobe activity) causes durable microbiota perturbations.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"Section 5.2 and Table 3 summarize overall antibiotic impact on ICI efficacy across histologies (Routy, Derosa, Pinato, etc.), supporting the general negative association. Section 5.4 and Table 4 discuss timing windows (30 days before/after initiation) which may confound class-specific effects. Section 8 Discussion highlights the difficulty in extrapolating preclinical data and the presence of indication biases.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied material identifies studies that compared antibiotic classes but reports conflicting conclusions (Ahmed et al. suggest narrow-spectrum is safer, while Chalabi et al. and Gaucher et al. found no significant class-specific differences). The specific head-to-head comparison between β-lactams and fluoroquinolones is not definitively resolved as they are often grouped under 'broad-spectrum' in the cited analyses. A consensus on which specific class is 'relatively safe' is not established.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Use this review to cite the existence of class-comparison studies (Ahmed, Chalabi, Gaucher) and the prevailing view that 'broad-spectrum' activity (particularly against anaerobes) is the primary risk factor rather than a specific chemical class. Acknowledge the conflicting evidence in the review's discussion. For the planning gap, note that while ≥2 comparative studies are identified via this review, their discordant results mean the gap question (is there a significant difference/safe class?) remains partially unanswered, supporting the alternative success criterion path that existing material primarily supports a 'broad-spectrum' overall effect warning.\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to analyze a captured paper text (a review article) against a specific gap question about antibiotic class specificity in ICI efficacy.\n\n**Gap Question:** \"不同类别抗生素（如β-内酰胺类 vs. 氟喹诺酮类）对 ICI 疗效的负面影响是否存在显著差异，是否有特定类别相对安全？\" (Are there significant differences in the negative impact on ICI efficacy between different classes of antibiotics (e.g., beta-lactams vs. fluoroquinolones)? Is there a specific class that is relatively safe?)\n\n**Success Criteria:**\n1. Find at least 2 studies comparing different antibiotic classes' impact on ICI efficacy, OR\n2. Explicitly state that existing material only supports the 'broad-spectrum antibiotic' overall effect.\n\n**Task:**\n1. Describe what the supplied paper studied and found.\n2. Explain how that material may help with the requested gap.\n3. Determine status (fulfilled, partial, unmet, unjudgeable).\n4. Determine material_usefulness (direct, supporting, none).\n5. Identify useful_material and auxiliary_material.\n6. Describe remaining_gap and outline_action.\n\n**Analysis of the Supplied Text:**\n- The text is a review article (\"In this review, we analyzed the existing data...\").\n- It discusses the impact of various comedications (corticosteroids, antibiotics, PPIs, etc.) on ICI efficacy.\n- Section 5 focuses on Antibiotics.\n- Section 5.1 discusses antibiotic-induced perturbations of microbiota (mentions clindamycin, clarithromycin, metronidazole in context of microbiota changes, not specifically ICI efficacy comparison).\n- Section 5.2 discusses impact on ICI response according to histology (Routy, Derosa, Pinato, Tinsley, Cortellini studies). These generally look at \"antibiotics\" as a group or \"broad-spectrum\".\n- Section 5.5 is titled \"Importance of the Antibiotic Treatment Modality: Molecule and Spectrum\".\n    - It states: \"In vitro studies showed maximal impact on microbiota with antibiotics having a strong activity on anaerobes (e.g., macrolides).\"\n    - It mentions: \"Some studies have been able to differentiate the action of antibiotics on the results of immunotherapy according to their class.\"\n    - **Ahmed et al. [78]:** \"showed no impact on response rate with narrow Gram-positive spectrum antibiotics (vancomycin, daptomycin, linezolid), whereas broad spectrum antibiotics (e.g., β–lactams, quinolones, cyclines) affected both response rate and PFS.\" -> This is a direct comparison of classes/spectra.\n    - **Chalabi et al. [79]:** \"examined the difference in OS between chemotherapy and atezolizumab (anti-PD-L1) for several antibiotic classes without showing any significantly impairing class for ICIs survival.\" -> This suggests no significant difference found in their analysis.\n    - **Gaucher et al. [6]:** \"found no difference in OS between patients treated with AMX versus AMC, nor between those treated with AMX or AMC versus other antibiotics.\" -> Comparison within beta-lactams and vs others.\n    - **Medik et al. [80]:** Mice treated with anti-CTLA-4 and metronidazole showed favorable immune microenvironment. (Preclinical).\n- The text *cites* these studies but does not provide the full data of those original studies. It summarizes their findings.\n- The gap asks for \"at least 2 studies comparing different antibiotic classes\". The review *mentions* Ahmed et al. and Chalabi et al. and Gaucher et al. as having looked at this.\n- However, the success criteria says \"Find at least 2 studies... OR explicitly state that existing material only supports 'broad-spectrum antibiotic' overall effect.\"\n- Since this is a *review* paper, it summarizes other studies. The prompt instructions say: \"Review-reported original studies remain usable writing material with their review attribution; do not claim the original full text was read.\"\n- So I can use the review's report of Ahmed et al. and Chalabi et al. as evidence that such comparisons exist in the literature, but the review itself concludes mixed results (Ahmed